-- #1111 A-2.13 (#1319 unit 3) — #1377's NAMES-ONE-HOLDS-ANOTHER shape, with the
-- collision made OBSERVABLE.  Sibling of `record_receiver_ident_named_vs_held/`,
-- which asserts the same acceptance but CANNOT see a layout error: its two
-- colliding records have one field each, so both layouts pick index 0.
--
-- 🚨 THIS PAIR IS THE ROUND-2 REVIEW'S STRUCTURAL FINDING TURNED INTO A GATE.  The
-- review of PR #1381 observed that giving the colliding records disjoint or
-- differently-typed fields — done to make a wrong pick LOUD at the FRONT end —
-- is exactly what blinds the cell to a wrong pick in the BACK end.  Both records
-- here declare `shared`, at index 1 (`armod`) and index 0 (`zrmod`), so a wrong
-- pick is a wrong NUMBER on the executed binary.  Keep both cells: the single-field
-- one still grades the acceptance and the `Field 'beta' does not belong` direction,
-- and this one grades the layout.
--
-- ⚠️ EVAL IS A KNOWN-WRONG ORACLE FOR THE LAYOUT HALF, in the reassuring direction
-- — the interpreter resolves fields by NAME at run time — so the golden is
-- hand-derived and the cell is graded on the EXECUTED NATIVE (and WasmGC) BINARY by
-- `diff_compiler_engines.sh`'s module arm.
--
-- Hand-derived from the sources, before any engine was run:
--   `useA c = 0` by definition;
--   `mkA = Cfg { alpha = 1, shared = 2 }` => `mkA.alpha = 1`, `mkA.shared = 2`;
--   `mkZ = Cfg { shared = 7, beta = 5 }`  => `mkZ.shared = 7`, `mkZ.beta = 5`.
-- so `0,1,2,7,5`.  Applying armod's layout to `mkZ` would print `…,5,…`.
import remod.{Cfg, mkA}
import zrmod.{mkZ}

useA : Cfg -> Int
useA c = 0

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  putStrLn
    (intToString (useA mkA)
      ++ ","
      ++ intToString mkA.alpha
      ++ ","
      ++ intToString mkA.shared
      ++ ","
      ++ intToString mkZ.shared
      ++ ","
      ++ intToString mkZ.beta)
